# cefont

Turn TTF/OTF fonts into [FontLibC](https://ce-programming.github.io/toolchain/libraries/fontlibc.html)
fonts for the TI-84 Plus CE.

    pip install -e .            # needs Python 3.9+, Pillow (fontTools optional)

## Command line

    cefont convert Bungee-Regular.ttf -s 16 --weight bold -o bungee16.fnt --preview "FILES NOTES"
    cefont preview bungee16.fnt -t "Settings"
    cefont build bungee16.fnt --carray bungee16.inc            # needs convfont on PATH
    cefont build bungee16.fnt --pack bungee.bin --name Bungee  # then: convhex -a -v bungee.bin BUNGEE.8xv

`convert` renders hinted 1-bit glyphs (`--mode aa` renders antialiased and thresholds it),
trims the height to the ink, and sets baseline / cap height / x-height. FontLibC glyphs are
8-bit, so `--range` only accepts code points 0-255.

## Library

    from cefont import from_ttf, Font

    font = from_ttf("Bungee-Regular.ttf", 16, weight="bold")
    print("\n".join(font.render("Hello")))     # list of '#'/' ' rows, font.height tall
    open("bungee16.fnt", "w").write(font.to_fnt())
    font2 = Font.from_fnt(open("bungee16.fnt").read())   # read it back
    font.text_width("Hello"), font.baseline, font.glyphs[ord("A")].rows

The `Font` / `Glyph` model is what a font tester can build on.

## Tests

    pip install -e .[dev] && pytest
